00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:32.390
But let's talk about how discovery works

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.880
and how this person found
themselves in this situation.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.000
In civil discovery, one side
sends document requests.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.760
Those are allowed under the Federal Rules

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.680
of Civil Procedure, Rule 34.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.640
These requests have to specify exactly

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.740
what you're looking for, I mean right down

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.330
to every single word that
is used in the request,

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.040
because if there's any ambiguity,

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.770
it's going to cause problems later on.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:57.100
The other side can object,
and if they continue to object

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.070
and refuse to comply, then you
might have to go into court

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:01.930
and get an order forcing the other side

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:03.980
to comply with your document request,

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.080
which is yet another
reason why the request

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.820
should be worded correctly,
because if you didn't

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.850
word them correctly, then the other side

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.090
might not have to comply.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.610
Now that's just between
two parties to a lawsuit.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.410
Like a plaintiff and a defendant.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.860
But if you're trying to get
documents from a third party,

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.060
then you have to issue
what's called a subpoena.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.210
Now, a subpoena is
basically the same thing

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.797
as a Rule 34 document request,
it's just that there are

00:04:25.797 --> 00:04:28.404
a couple of extra strings
attached because you're trying

00:04:28.404 --> 00:04:31.930
to get documents from
someone who has nothing to do

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:33.480
with the lawsuit
whatsoever, so it's gonna be

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.960
a little bit more difficult.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.170
But generally, you can get the court

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:37.640
to force them to comply as well.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:39.610
Now, as I said, it's really important

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.220
for the document request
to be very, very specific

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:43.540
because the lawyers on the other side

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.440
are going to scrutinize your request.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.630
And if there is any way to get
out of that document request,

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:49.463
they will find it.

00:04:49.463 --> 00:04:51.530
So often, you will have specific
language in your request

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:54.260
that includes
electronically-stored information

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.550
like metadata, or you'll
just have an agreement

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.530
with the other side to deal with ESI.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:02.250
Back in the day, document
requests just meant

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.200
requesting actual, physical documents.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.720
But now, it's almost always
electronic documents,

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.790
things like emails and databases.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:12.210
And as a result, the federal courts

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.870
and lots of different states
have created rules for dealing

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:17.600
with electronically-stored
information like metadata.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.090
In case you aren't familiar with metadata,

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.440
it's described as the data about data.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.100
If you think about an
email that you receive,

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.810
that email will have a subject line.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:27.760
It will have to, from, BCC.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.290
It'll have the body of the email

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:31.180
and the text of that email is searchable.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:33.860
All of that would be considered
electronically-stored

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:37.400
information and much of that
would be considered metadata.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.790
Now imagine if you took
a picture of your email,

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:42.700
and then you sent that
picture to someone else.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.500
Well, you wouldn't be
able to sort that email

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.160
by the to-line or the
from-line or the subject line.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.440
And the computer can't search
the text of that picture

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.400
because it's just one
block of information.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.390
So, the metadata can be
incredibly important.

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:01.280
It can tell you when things were sent.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:03.400
When things were received.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:07.440
It can tell you what's inside
of the document itself.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.160
So it can be, not only important
for a particular lawsuit,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:14.760
but it can also make a
lawsuit so much less expensive

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.900
in terms of searching
things, because if you have

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.410
just a bunch of emails,
then you can sort them,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:20.380
you can search them, you can do

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:21.660
all of that stuff very easily.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.690
You'll see on TV often,
that the other side will try

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.700
to bury the other side in documents,

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.410
and they'll just send a truckload over

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:30.100
of physical paperwork.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.940
And sometimes that does happen.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.520
Courts are savvy to
that now, and if you try

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:37.117
and pull that crap, you just
go into court and you say,

00:06:37.117 --> 00:06:39.067
"Look, it's ridiculous that
they're trying to give us

00:06:39.067 --> 00:06:41.677
"paper documents when it would
have been easier for them

00:06:41.677 --> 00:06:44.537
"to turn over just a hard
drive full of information,

00:06:44.537 --> 00:06:47.457
"and we're not gonna go
through the expense of scanning

00:06:47.457 --> 00:06:49.930
"all of this information,
and then searching it."

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:52.450
So, what you see on TV
has a grain of truth,

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:54.380
but if you're a good
litigator, you should be able

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.020
to avoid circumstances like that.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:59.200
Now in the circumstances that
are set out by ParaillegalIT,

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.980
it's unclear how much
data has been subpoenaed.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.150
But if the employer feels that it would be

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:07.910
overly burdensome to
have to respond to it,

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.980
the correct method of dealing with that

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:13.440
is not to delete the
documents and the information.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:14.640
The correct way of dealing with that

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.480
is called a motion to
quash, which is basically,

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:18.077
just a motion, you go into
the court and you say,

00:07:18.077 --> 00:07:19.677
"It's ridiculous that we
have to comply with this.

00:07:19.677 --> 00:07:22.017
"We're not parties to this proceeding,

00:07:22.017 --> 00:07:24.797
"so why should we have to undergo the cost

00:07:24.797 --> 00:07:27.550
"of responding to these
ridiculous document requests?"

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.797
But, the way I read this
post is that it sounds

00:07:29.797 --> 00:07:31.910
like the documents have been identified

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:33.720
and they are responsive to the subpoena

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.030
and it's not overly
burdensome to respond to them.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:38.090
So that means that if
this person goes through

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.330
and destroys that evidence, that's called

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:43.900
spoliation of evidence, i.e.
the destruction of evidence.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:47.500
And, as you can guess,
that is in fact illegal.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:50.090
If the court finds out
that this company has

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:53.190
destroyed evidence, there are
probably three different kinds

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:55.420
of sanctions that it would
issue against the company,

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:56.820
and potentially, this individual.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:59.800
First are issue/inference sanctions.

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:01.580
Second are monetary sanctions,

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:04.480
and third is litigation-ending sanctions.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.210
Destroying the metadata could
easily lead to sanctions

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:08.760
by the trial court based on the assumption

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.090
that the employer had something to hide.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:13.760
And as the employer has effectively hidden

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:17.100
whatever that was, the court
would be forced to assume

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.426
that whatever the other
party alleged was true.

00:08:19.426 --> 00:08:21.980
This could lead to what's
called an issue sanction,

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.247
meaning that a jury, or the court

00:08:24.247 --> 00:08:25.970
in this particular incidence,

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.670
would be instructed that
whatever was at issue,

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:32.280
had been determined in
favor of the other side.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:33.600
Affectively, this would be like

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:37.010
a court-required admission
by the other side.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:38.870
This would of course, negate any advantage

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:40.660
that the employer thought he might gain

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:42.020
by destroying the data.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.290
When you engage in these
kind of shenanigans,

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.740
what tends to happen is
the court orders you to pay

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:49.330
the costs and fees of the other side

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:51.910
for having to file a motion for sanctions,

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:54.890
or to try and recreate the
data that you destroyed.

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:56.570
Those are called monetary sanctions.

00:08:56.570 --> 00:09:00.520
And if your conduct is bad
enough, then you can get

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.560
what are called
litigation-ending sanctions,

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:05.307
where the court just
says, "You have engaged

00:09:05.307 --> 00:09:07.417
"in such a nefarious
act, that we're going to

00:09:07.417 --> 00:09:09.800
"end this suit and find against you."

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.390
criminal sanctions are possible,

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:13.250
but they're pretty unlikely.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:15.846
We've seen spoliation of
evidence in a few high-profile

00:09:15.846 --> 00:09:18.701
cases like the Waymo
versus Uber litigation,

00:09:18.701 --> 00:09:21.440
where they dealt with
a trade secret dispute

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:24.320
over an employee that left
from one employer to the other.

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:27.050
And in that case, the
plaintiff sort of stakes

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:30.300
the outcome of the case
on Uber's destruction

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:33.546
of text messages, saying
that because Uber destroyed

00:09:33.546 --> 00:09:37.140
these relevant text messages,
that the court had to make

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:40.620
a negative inference
against Uber, and therefore,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:43.170
basically the other side
should have won the case.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:45.410
Now generally, you can
only get sanctions against

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:49.090
the other side when they have
engaged in willful destruction

00:09:49.090 --> 00:09:52.713
or sometimes, grossly negligent
destruction of evidence.

00:09:52.713 --> 00:09:55.070
That's why an attorney
will send what's called

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:57.880
a litigation hold letter
to inform the other side

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.599
that they have to preserve documents,

00:09:59.599 --> 00:10:03.260
lest they be liable for
spoliation of evidence.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:06.350
So, what is this hapless
IT professional to do

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:07.720
under these circumstances?

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:09.190
Well, to figure that out, let's do

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.350
our very first comment review.

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:13.560
(rock music)

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:16.937
So, the first comment of note
comes from chivil61 that says,

00:10:16.937 --> 00:10:19.267
"First, are you being
asked to permanently delete

00:10:19.267 --> 00:10:21.687
"the metadata, or just
remove it from copies

00:10:21.687 --> 00:10:24.040
"that will be produced in
response to the subpoena?"

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:26.080
This is an excellent observation,

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:27.547
and we'll get to that in just a second.

00:10:27.547 --> 00:10:29.317
"Permanently deleting metadata is likely

00:10:29.317 --> 00:10:31.800
"spoliation of evidence,
and is a huge no-no."

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:32.977
Absolutely correct.

00:10:32.977 --> 00:10:35.327
"However, if you are simply
removing the metadata

00:10:35.327 --> 00:10:37.677
"from the files before
production, it could be okay,

00:10:37.677 --> 00:10:39.207
"depending on the format in which

00:10:39.207 --> 00:10:41.170
"the files have been subpoenaed."

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:43.690
So, what this person is pointing out is,

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:45.949
going back to what I stated
at the very beginning

00:10:45.949 --> 00:10:48.590
of this video, which
is that you have to be

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:51.540
incredibly precise in terms
of what you are asking for.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.030
And often, you'll have
a separate agreement

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:57.390
about what to do with ESI like metadata.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:58.729
What this person is pointing out is that,

00:10:58.729 --> 00:11:02.440
perhaps this IT professional
has misunderstood

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:04.895
what the boss is asking,
and what's very common

00:11:04.895 --> 00:11:07.380
in litigation is that if you have agreed

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:10.600
to turn over electronic
documents without metadata,

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:11.760
then you would strip it out

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:14.120
before you turn it over to the other side.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:17.280
And assuming that there is
some agreement to that affect,

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:20.570
that's totally okay,
that's absolutely kosher.

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:23.430
However, in almost any
circumstances that I can think of,

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:25.760
if you have been instructed
to permanently delete

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:28.020
the metadata off of your computer,

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:30.123
or the company's computer,
such as it can never

00:11:30.123 --> 00:11:33.540
be recovered, that's definitely
spoliation of evidence.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:36.130
So, getting that kind of
clarification before completely

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:38.380
freaking out would
probably be a good idea.

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:40.637
Now user naturalborncitizen writes,

00:11:40.637 --> 00:11:42.367
"I would definitely get this in writing,

00:11:42.367 --> 00:11:45.295
"i.e. 'Just to confirm, are
you requesting that I am to

00:11:45.295 --> 00:11:48.177
"'delete all metadata from
these subpoenaed files, or just

00:11:48.177 --> 00:11:51.467
"'certain fields, and if only
certain fields, which ones?'

00:11:51.467 --> 00:11:53.667
"Then print and save
the response somewhere,

00:11:53.667 --> 00:11:56.132
"and if possible, find somebody
at the applicable court

00:11:56.132 --> 00:11:57.556
"to send a copy to."

00:11:57.556 --> 00:12:01.510
Now I'm a little weary about
sending it to the court,

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:04.080
but it is almost always a good idea

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:05.800
to get things in writing.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:07.780
Not only is it a good
idea to get it in writing

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:10.310
from the particular
boss to make absolutely,

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:12.240
crystal clear that he
is asking this person

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.750
to delete permanently, the metadata,

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.670
but he might even copy
the corporate counsel

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.500
and make sure that the counsel is aware

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:24.500
of potentially illegal
spoliation of evidence.

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:25.920
This might do a couple of things.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.740
Number one, the thing about lawyers is

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:30.470
that we tend to not like illegal things

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:32.780
going on under our noses.

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:34.990
So if it's brought to
the lawyer's attention,

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:37.000
and it is what this person says it is,

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.100
number one, he'll get
clarification of what he's

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:42.450
being asked to do, and number
two, he may have someone

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:45.285
champion his cause and
intervene to try and prevent

00:12:45.285 --> 00:12:47.670
the illegal destruction of evidence.

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:49.470
And there's probably also a third reason

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:51.830
why it would be very, very
important for this person

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:54.320
to get this instruction in writing.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:56.270
Most states have laws
that protect employees

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:57.900
who refuse to do illegal things,

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.270
and then are fired by their employer.

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:02.502
And the only thing better
than doing the right thing

00:13:02.502 --> 00:13:05.410
is having a paper trail
of doing the right thing.

